{
  "benchmark": "GoodAI LTM Benchmark",
  "source": "github.com/GoodAI/goodai-ltm-benchmark \u2014 data/tests/'Benchmark 3 - 32k'/definitions (the published v3 set: 11 tests \u00d7 3 stored instances)",
  "triaged": "2026-09-13",
  "sampling": "all 33 stored definitions of the published 32k benchmark, no selection; the 120k/200k/500k variants store the same 11 tests with more filler between statements. Restaurant is a dynamic test whose script is generated at run time and not stored \u2014 labelled from its generator. Four unpublished generators (Conflicting Personal Information, Names, Locations, Instruction Recall, KV) were read but produce no stored instances and are not counted.",
  "decision_rules_frozen_before_reading": {
    "finding_1_testable_if": "temporal_update_count >= 10",
    "finding_2_testable_if": "provenance_sensitive_count >= 5",
    "finding_3_testable_if": "type_dependent_count >= 5 (same_surface_different_semantics)",
    "questions_this_triage_answers": [
      "A: is GoodAI only newest-wins?",
      "B: does anything require historical AND current state?",
      "C: is there any source or type semantics?"
    ]
  },
  "counts": {
    "classification": {
      "other": 12,
      "temporal_update": 9,
      "static_recall": 6,
      "historical_state": 6
    },
    "temporal_update_count": 15,
    "of_which_newest_wins_sufficient": 6,
    "of_which_requires_previous_state": 6,
    "provenance_sensitive_count": 0,
    "instances_with_multiple_sources_present": 3,
    "type_dependent_count": 0,
    "static_or_other": 18
  },
  "answers": {
    "A_only_newest_wins": "No. Of 15 state-change instances, 6 are newest-wins (Colours, Restaurant), 6 require every superseded value (NameList) or the value as of an earlier observation (SallyAnne), and 3 are accumulation (Shopping) \u2014 a semantics that is neither.",
    "B_historical_and_current_together": "Yes \u2014 NameList asks for every value the slot has ever held, in order; SallyAnne asks for the value as of the moment an observer last saw it. Both are as-of / history questions on a replaced slot, on data designed before TypedMem existed. Together with LongMemEval's paired initial/current questions this is a second independent source for the replace-in-place known gap.",
    "C_source_or_type_semantics": "Sources: present in Spy Meeting (three named senders) and Restaurant (user + waiter), but gold never depends on which source said what \u2192 0 provenance-sensitive. Types: Shopping's set-valued accumulation is a genuinely different update semantics from Colours' scalar replacement, so type-dependent semantics exist in the benchmark \u2014 but never as a controlled contrast on one surface form, and no instance's gold turns on choosing between them \u2192 0 by the frozen rule."
  },
  "verdict": {
    "finding_1_bitemporal_sufficient": "TESTABLE \u2014 15 state-change instances (threshold 10); and the mix is more informative than LongMemEval's: it contains newest-wins, retain-all-history, and accumulate, so a bitemporal baseline is predicted to win NameList and SallyAnne outright and to need a fold for Shopping.",
    "finding_2_confidence_vs_authority": "NOT TESTABLE \u2014 0 provenance-sensitive instances. The unpublished 'Conflicting Personal Information' generator is the nearest thing anywhere found (a generalisation vs. a personal exception), but it is preference reconciliation within one speaker, GPT-generated, and has no stored instances.",
    "finding_3_type_dependent_resolution": "NOT TESTABLE by the frozen rule \u2014 0 same-surface contrasts. Recorded as evidence that a third semantics (accumulation) occurs naturally and that TypedMem's replace / keep_both pair does not cover it.",
    "adapter": "Track E-A is feasible for 8 of 11 tests: statements are templated (Colours, NameList, Shopping, Locations, Spy Meeting, Trigger, Prospective) so writes derive deterministically without an LLM. Restaurant is dynamic and needs a replay harness; ChapterBreak and Jokes are out of scope. Interleaving with filler is the benchmark's retrieval stressor and is irrelevant to oracle-candidate state evaluation.",
    "caveat": "Instances are generated from templates with random values, so the 33 are 11 structures \u00d7 3 draws. Counting them as 33 overstates diversity; counting them as 11 understates the benchmark's own unit. Both numbers are reported."
  },
